\section{Recover the Types That the Ellipsis Does Not State}

A declaration such as \texttt{int emit(const char *format, ...)}
supplies a type for the fixed prefix but not for its tail. An
invocation bridge needs both: the ABI cannot place an argument
whose native type it does not know. In addition, the values passed
in the variadic tail undergo C's default argument promotions.
An input \texttt{float} becomes \texttt{double}; smaller-rank
integers undergo integer promotions, which commonly produce
\texttt{int} but can produce \texttt{unsigned int} if
\texttt{int} cannot represent every value of the original type.
These are conversions, not instructions to copy a narrow
object into one end of a larger argument slot.

The callee also needs a way to know what to fetch. It may read
a format, consume a fixed sequence of types determined by a
command, or use a count or typed sentinel. A count alone cannot
describe a heterogeneous sequence. An arbitrary-precision host
integer has no unique native width chosen by the C ellipsis.
Infer native types only from a validated operation-specific
schema. Where an API uses a null pointer sentinel, stage an
actual null of the prescribed pointer type, not an integer
zero with whatever size and passing class the host prefers.

\section{Compare Two x64 Call Plans for One Tail}

For the example, declare that the known schema consumes a promoted
double followed by an int. The fixed format is a native pointer.
Use \(1.25\) and \(7\) as the tail values. The System V AMD64
supplement \cite{amd64abi2014} uses independent integer and vector argument registers
and specifies a hidden value in \texttt{al} for variadic calls:
an upper bound on the number of vector registers used, between
zero and eight. The bridge can use the exact count. On the
Microsoft x64 convention \cite{microsoftx64calling}, the first four positions use register
slots by position, with floating-point values duplicated in the
corresponding general-purpose registers for variadic calls. The
local Windows assembly extract describes the register slots,
alignment and caller-reserved home area.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Value or obligation & System V AMD64 & Microsoft x64 \\
\hline
Format pointer & \texttt{rdi} & \texttt{rcx} \\
Promoted double & \texttt{xmm0} & \texttt{xmm1} and \texttt{rdx} \\
Promoted integer & \texttt{esi} & \texttt{r8d} \\
Variadic setup & \texttt{al} may be 1 & FP duplication as above \\
Home area for this call & None required & 32 bytes reserved by caller \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Duplication is a bit copy of the double's representation for
placing the argument; it is not a numeric conversion to an
integer. The home area is separate from any padding needed for
stack alignment and from arguments passed on the stack. On
System V, a variadic fixed prefix containing a vector-register
argument contributes to the register count too. The tail types
alone are not enough to compute that count.

\Cref{fig:visual-393} uses a fork to show which information is
shared and which is target-specific. C promotion precedes both
placement decisions. Conversely, a callee's \texttt{va\_list}
is not a portable array of word-sized arguments: the callee
may have to consult register-save areas and overflow storage.

