% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Validate a Closed Family of Variadic Shapes}

\begin{verbatim}
emit_checked(operation, values, target):
    schema := supported_operations.lookup(operation)
    require(schema is not None)
    require(length(values) == length(schema.tail))
    fixed := [stable_native_format(schema.format_literal)]
    tail := []
    for expected, supplied in zip(schema.tail, values):
        narrow := checked_convert(supplied, expected.source_c_type)
        promoted := default_argument_promotion(narrow, target)
        require(promoted.c_type == expected.fetched_c_type)
        tail.append(promoted)
    shape := signature(int_result, types(fixed), types(tail))
    plan := prepare_variadic(target, fixed_count=1, shape=shape)
    return plan.call(emit_entry, concatenate(fixed, tail))
\end{verbatim}

Validate under the semantic types first. If the operation wants
a finite measurement representable as native \texttt{float},
validate and round to that type before its promotion to
\texttt{double}; using the unrounded host real would give
different semantics. If the operation instead accepts a C
double directly, do not round to float just because the
ellipsis will ultimately use the same ABI class. The format
string here is selected by the wrapper, not parsed from an
arbitrary caller-supplied format with hidden width and precision
arguments. A fixed-signature C shim for each allowed schema is
often simpler than dynamic variadic preparation. The compiled
shim must call the prototype including its ellipsis.

\section{Test Both Register and Overflow Paths}

Build a native receiver that reads the tail in the promised
order and stores a typed trace. Compare it with a direct C
caller. Exercise an empty tail, a tail starting with a
promoted integer followed by a promoted float, a tail with
more than four total arguments on Microsoft x64, and one that
uses all the System V vector slots before spilling. Use an
exactly representable noninteger in each floating position
and distinct integer patterns in the intervening positions.
Tests that fetch only the first word cannot distinguish several
incorrect placement algorithms.

On the callee side, build a second fixture that delegates to
a supported \texttt{v} variant using a \texttt{va\_list}
initialized by the target C compiler. A function taking a
\texttt{va\_list} is not interchangeable with the original
variadic function; its explicit last parameter has its own
target-dependent type. Forward through C when forwarding itself
is needed. Do not construct a \texttt{va\_list} by converting
an array of prepared cells into a pointer.

\section{Exercise a Tail Whose Values Have Different Origins}

Suppose an operation expects a count of small bins, a small
real-valued offset, and a marker pointer. On a target with
32-bit \texttt{int}, its tail specification can start with
an unsigned 8-bit count and a \texttt{float} offset, but the
receiver must fetch \texttt{int}, \texttt{double}, and the
specified pointer type. The source count 255 is valid and
becomes a positive \texttt{int}; 256 is rejected before
promoting even though a native int could hold it. The
offset is rounded to the source float representation and
then widened. A zero host integer cannot be accepted as
the marker without an explicit conversion to the pointer
sentinel the schema documents. These choices distinguish
the domain of accepted values from the fetching types.

\begin{verbatim}
receive_bin_trace(tag, ...):
    cursor := target_va_start(tag)
    try:
        count := target_va_arg(cursor, C_INT)
        offset := target_va_arg(cursor, C_DOUBLE)
        marker := target_va_arg(cursor, C_VOID_POINTER)
        return encode_trace(tag, count, offset, marker == native_null)
    finally:
        target_va_end(cursor)
\end{verbatim}

Compile the receiver in C rather than simulating native
\texttt{va\_arg} with this pseudocode. For this example the
C shim declares the fixed \texttt{tag} parameter with a
type suitable for its environment's \texttt{va\_start}
interface. If two consumers need independent traversals of
the tail, use the target's \texttt{va\_copy} and give each
cursor its own \texttt{va\_end}; a plain assignment is not
portable across representations. The wrapper should not
export the cursor beyond the receiver's lifetime. A safer
public result is the copied trace of already fetched values,
which has no dependency on incoming argument storage.

For the Microsoft fixture, place a floating-point argument
past the fourth position and check it as well as the
duplicated register cases. For the System V fixture, put a
fixed double before the tail doubles and check that the
call plan counts it along with the tail's vector arguments.
These tests detect a bridge that implemented the tail
promotion rules but forgot that the fixed prefix participates
in call classification and can exhaust register capacity.

If the binding caches these plans, key the cache by target,
convention, return type, fixed count and the complete sequence
of effective argument types. Two calls with the same total
arity may have different fixed counts and need different
preparation. An unsupported schema should fail before lookup
or invocation rather than reuse a plan for a superficially
similar sequence of register classes.
